182313	281891	\N	Female	+91 9804424848	\N	GEETA CHHATRI	\N	ACE (ANGIOTENSIN CONVERTING ENZYME)	Pathology	07-06-2026 11:26	09-06-2026	07-06-2026	495743	\N	\N	bgl-Madhav	Descriptive (No Ranges)	-	07-06-2026	09-06-2026	&nbsp;	Comments:\nAngiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) modulates peripheral vascular resistance as well as renal and cardiovascular function. It is responsible for conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II as well as inactivation of bradykinin . Majority of ACE is tissue bound (> 90%) found predominantly in lungs.\n\n \n\nFactors affecting ACE levels:\n\n1. Smoking - ACE activity is 30% lower in smokers.\n2. ACE levels are significantly reduced in patients on ACE inhibitors (e.g. enalapril & captopril).\n3. Thyroid hormone- Stimulates ACE synthesis. 4. Postmenopausal estrogen replacement - ACE activity is 20% lower.\n\n4. Postmenopausal estrogen replacement - ACE activity is 20% lower.\n\n \n\nUse:\n1. Evaluation of patients with suspected sarcoidosis.\n2. Evaluate the severity and activity of sarcoidosis.\n3. Evaluation of hypertension.\n4. Evaluation of Gaucher disease.	RGH YERWADA NPMC	\N
182313	281891	\N	Female	+91 9804424848	\N	GEETA CHHATRI	\N	ACE (ANGIOTENSIN CONVERTING ENZYME)	Pathology	07-06-2026 11:26	09-06-2026	07-06-2026	495743	\N	\N	bgl-Madhav	Descriptive (No Ranges)	-	07-06-2026	09-06-2026	&nbsp;	Increased levels:\n1. Sarcoidosis - ACE levels are used in the diagnosis and monitoring of this disease & are directly related to number of organs affected and activity of granulomas. Mature granulomas produce less ACE than developing ones. ACE is more likely to be elevated with pulmonary involvement than with purely hilar adenopathy. ACE activity reflects the severity of Sarcoidosis showing 68% positivity in stage I of the disease, 86% in stage II and 91% in stage III.\n2. Gaucher disease. (100%)\n3. Chronic Renal disease - patients on hemodialysis show high ACE levels as compared to patients who are not on dialysis.\n4. Other causes - Addisons disease, Hyperthyroidism, Diabetes, Cirrhosis, Leprosy, Hyperthyroidism, Amyloidosis, Histoplasmosis, Berylliosis.\n\n\nDecreased levels:\n1. Far-advanced lung neoplasm.\n2. Anorexia nervosa, starvation\n3. Hypothyroidism\n4. COPD, emphysema, lung cancer, cystic fibrosis	RGH YERWADA NPMC	\N
